  "mathematical_conditions": [
    "Working domain: real terms, real common ratios, and real-valued square roots/domains. This is an explicit interpretation of the supplied elementary algebra and ordered sign conditions, not an assumption that complex ratios are permitted.",
    "Definition blocks 1 and 4 require the first term and common ratio to be nonzero. By induction every term of a geometric sequence is nonzero.",
    "Blocks 3 and 4 give b_(n+1)/b_n=q and b_n=b_1*q^(n-1). Hence b_(n+r)=b_n*q^r for positive integer r; division and backward recovery are valid because the terms and q are nonzero.",
    "An even index gap determines an even power of q, not necessarily its sign. A positive value of q^2 gives two real nonzero ratios; a negative value gives no real ratio. An odd gap such as q^3 fixes one real ratio.",
    "Block 7 permits negative ratios and their alternating signs; it supplies no license to discard a negative branch when only two same-parity terms are known. Block 9 permits q=1, giving a nonzero constant sequence.",
    "For the nonzero sequences at issue, the proportional-middle relation in blocks 10 and 16 is b_n^2=b_(n-1)*b_(n+1). This also follows directly from the recurrence, so no unseen definition is needed. Conversely, on nonzero terms that relation makes the consecutive ratios equal. It is not being applied to a zero sequence.",
    "Increasing/decreasing in exercise 276 is strict for every n. With q>0, all b_n have the sign of b_1 and b_(n+1)-b_n=b_n*(q-1).",
    "For exercise 277, every radicand must be >=0; a square root occurring in a denominator must have radicand >0; every other denominator must be nonzero. The domain is the intersection of all conditions, with endpoints tested individually.",
    "The alternatives in 273(b), 274(a), and 274(c) and the inconsistency in 274(b) are complete source conclusions, not pending guesses or candidate verdicts."
  ],
