    {
      "source_number": "273",
      "subquestion_count": 3,
      "solution_status": "complete",
      "independent_solution": "(a) q=x_2/x_1, since x_2=x_1*q and x_1!=0. (b) x_3=x_1*q^2, so q^2=x_3/x_1>0 and the complete possible real set is {sqrt(x_3/x_1),-sqrt(x_3/x_1)}. The given two terms do not select a sign; either choice produces a geometric sequence through them with x_2=x_1*q. For example x_1=1,x_3=4 permits 1,2,4,... with q=2 and 1,-2,4,... with q=-2. Data with x_1=0, x_3=0, or x_3/x_1<=0 cannot be such real geometric terms. (c) q=x_(k+1)/x_k for any positive integer k because all x_k are nonzero.",
      "subquestions": [
        {"label": "a", "q": "x_2/x_1", "conditions": ["x_1!=0", "x_2!=0"], "derivation": "Divide x_2=x_1*q by x_1."},
        {"label": "b", "q_complete_set": ["sqrt(x_3/x_1)", "-sqrt(x_3/x_1)"], "conditions": ["x_1!=0", "x_3/x_1>0"], "uniqueness": "not unique without extra sign information", "derivation": "q^2=x_3/x_1. Both nonzero real square roots satisfy the two-term data; x_2=x_1*q reconstructs each branch."},
        {"label": "c", "q": "x_(k+1)/x_k", "conditions": ["k is a positive integer", "x_k!=0", "x_(k+1)!=0"], "derivation": "Divide x_(k+1)=x_k*q by x_k."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "source_number": "274",
      "subquestion_count": 4,
      "solution_status": "complete_including_inconsistent_real_data",
      "independent_solution": "(a) q^2=a_5/a_3=24/6=4. Both q=2 and q=-2 are allowed. Since a_6=a_5*q and a_7=a_5*q^2, the complete (q,a_6,a_7) tuples are (2,48,96) and (-2,-48,96). (b) q^2=a_8/a_6=-10/10=-1, impossible over the reals. Equivalently a_7^2=a_6*a_8=-100 is impossible. Therefore no real geometric sequence satisfies these data; a_6=10 is a stated datum but there is no consistent real tuple (q,a_6,a_7). (c) q^2=a_5/a_3=(-18)/(-2)=9, so q=3 or q=-3. The complete tuples are (3,-54,-162) and (-3,54,-162). (d) q^3=a_8/a_5=3/24=1/8, whose unique real root is q=1/2. Thus a_6=12 and a_7=6; multiplication once more gives the required a_8=3. No positive-only restriction on q is supplied.",
