    {
      "source_number": "276",
      "subquestion_count": 4,
      "solution_status": "complete",
      "independent_solution": "Give an actual sequence in each case, then justify monotonicity for every sequence satisfying the case, not merely for a short prefix. Since q>0, b_n has the sign of b_1, and b_(n+1)-b_n=b_n*(q-1). (a) b_n=2^(n-1), beginning 1,2,4,8,..., has b_1=1>0,q=2>1; the difference is positive, so it is strictly increasing. (b) b_n=(1/2)^(n-1), beginning 1,1/2,1/4,1/8,..., has b_1=1>0,0<q=1/2<1; the difference is negative, so it is strictly decreasing. (c) b_n=-2^(n-1), beginning -1,-2,-4,-8,..., has b_1=-1<0,q=2>1; the difference is negative, so it is strictly decreasing. (d) b_n=-(1/2)^(n-1), beginning -1,-1/2,-1/4,-1/8,..., has b_1=-1<0,0<q=1/2<1; the difference is positive, so it is strictly increasing. All four examples have nonzero terms and ratios.",
      "subquestions": [
        {"label": "a", "example_general_term": "2^(n-1)", "example_first_four_terms": ["1", "2", "4", "8"], "b1": "1", "q": "2", "monotonicity": "strictly increasing", "all_n_proof": "b_n>0 and q-1>0, hence b_n*(q-1)>0."},
        {"label": "b", "example_general_term": "(1/2)^(n-1)", "example_first_four_terms": ["1", "1/2", "1/4", "1/8"], "b1": "1", "q": "1/2", "monotonicity": "strictly decreasing", "all_n_proof": "b_n>0 and q-1<0, hence b_n*(q-1)<0."},
        {"label": "c", "example_general_term": "-(2^(n-1))", "example_first_four_terms": ["-1", "-2", "-4", "-8"], "b1": "-1", "q": "2", "monotonicity": "strictly decreasing", "all_n_proof": "b_n<0 and q-1>0, hence b_n*(q-1)<0."},
        {"label": "d", "example_general_term": "-((1/2)^(n-1))", "example_first_four_terms": ["-1", "-1/2", "-1/4", "-1/8"], "b1": "-1", "q": "1/2", "monotonicity": "strictly increasing", "all_n_proof": "b_n<0 and q-1<0, hence b_n*(q-1)>0."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "source_number": "277",
      "subquestion_count": 4,
      "solution_status": "complete",
      "independent_solution": "(a) 2x-3>=0 and 3-2x>=0 force x>=3/2 and x<=3/2; domain {3/2}. Both roots equal zero there and are permitted. (b) 6x>=0 gives x>=0, while the square-root denominator requires 5x+1>0, or x>-1/5. Intersection: [0,+infinity); x=0 is included since the denominator is 1. (c) x^2-3x=x*(x-3)!=0 excludes 0 and 3, and 7-4x>=0 requires x<=7/4. Since 3 already lies outside that interval, the domain is (-infinity,0) union (0,7/4]. At x=7/4 the root is zero and the denominator is -35/16!=0, so the upper endpoint is included. (d) x^2-16=(x-4)*(x+4)!=0 excludes -4 and 4, while 10-2x>=0 requires x<=5. The domain is (-infinity,-4) union (-4,4) union (4,5]. At 5 the root is zero and the denominator is 9, so 5 is included; neither -4 nor 4 is included.",
      "subquestions": [
        {"label": "a", "conditions": ["2*x-3>=0", "3-2*x>=0"], "reduced_conditions": ["x>=3/2", "x<=3/2"], "domain_set": "{3/2}", "endpoint_evidence": "Both radicands equal zero at x=3/2; neither square root is a denominator."},
        {"label": "b", "conditions": ["6*x>=0", "5*x+1>0"], "reduced_conditions": ["x>=0", "x>-1/5"], "domain_interval": "[0,+infinity)", "endpoint_evidence": "At x=0 the radicands are 0 and 1, so the square-root denominator is nonzero. x=-1/5 is inadmissible and also fails x>=0."},
        {"label": "c", "conditions": ["x*(x-3)!=0", "7-4*x>=0"], "reduced_conditions": ["x!=0", "x!=3", "x<=7/4"], "domain_interval": "(-infinity,0) union (0,7/4]", "endpoint_evidence": "0 is a denominator zero. 3 is already outside x<=7/4. At x=7/4 the radicand is 0 and the denominator is -35/16, so 7/4 is included."},
        {"label": "d", "conditions": ["(x-4)*(x+4)!=0", "10-2*x>=0"], "reduced_conditions": ["x!=-4", "x!=4", "x<=5"], "domain_interval": "(-infinity,-4) union (-4,4) union (4,5]", "endpoint_evidence": "-4 and 4 make the denominator zero and are excluded. At x=5 the radicand is 0 and the denominator is 9, so 5 is included."}
      ]
    }
